Let NA_UCX_EXTRA_TLS add UCX memory-type components, defaulting to none.

WHY THIS DEFAULTS TO OFF -- READ BEFORE CHANGING IT

The first version of this patch appended "cuda_copy,cuda_ipc" unconditionally
(NA_UCX_EXTRA_TLS only overrode it). That silently corrupted ORDINARY
HOST-MEMORY bulk transfers on any client where CUDA is loadable: one 4 MiB
region per transfer came back wrong, only with 4 or more concurrent readers,
with sizes and return codes all normal. In the vLLM container CUDA always leads
LD_LIBRARY_PATH, so it was always on. Measured on client-6 -- one host, one
client bundle, one test, nothing else changed:

    CUDA ahead of DAOS on the library path, appended by default : FAIL
    CUDA ahead of DAOS on the library path, NA_UCX_EXTRA_TLS=   : PASS 80/80
    DAOS ahead of CUDA on the library path                      : PASS 80/80

So a patch whose whole purpose was enabling GPU-direct was breaking the path
that does not use the GPU. It is needed only for dfs_read_gpu()/dfs_write_gpu(),
and gpudirect/PLAN.md records the decision NOT to build the GPU-direct backend
(GPU-direct measured 2.09x slower than host staging at the chunk sizes LMCache
actually uses). Nothing in the production path wants it, so it is opt-in:

    unset / empty  -> UCX gets exactly the requested protocol, as upstream
    set to a list  -> that list is appended, e.g.
                      NA_UCX_EXTRA_TLS=cuda_copy,cuda_ipc

Set it only for GPU-direct experiments, and re-run tests/test_rawio_integrity.py
(28 MiB, 16 threads) afterwards, requiring a clean pass before trusting
anything measured with it. The failure mode is silent.

Existing trees: this replaces an earlier version of the same patch, so on a tree
carrying the OLD version apply-patches.sh reports "DOES NOT APPLY" rather than
quietly mixing the two. Reverse the old one, or rebuild the prereq from clean,
first.

Verified by generating the diff against a pristine na_ucx.c (old patch reversed)
rather than editing hunk counts by hand -- the first hand-written attempt was
malformed.

--- a/src/na/na_ucx.c
+++ b/src/na/na_ucx.c
@@ -1426,8 +1426,39 @@
         na_ucs_status_to_na(status), "ucp_config_read() failed (%s)",
         ucs_status_string(status));
 
-    /* Set user-requested transport */
-    status = ucp_config_modify(config, "TLS", tls);
+    /* Set user-requested transport, optionally with extra components.
+     *
+     * cuda_copy and cuda_ipc are memory-type components, not network
+     * transports, but UCX gates them through the same TLS list. Restricting
+     * TLS to just the requested protocol therefore drops the component that
+     * supplies the dma-buf file descriptor for GPU buffers, and a later
+     * ucp_mem_map() of CUDA memory fails with -EINVAL (it falls back to
+     * ibv_reg_mr() on a device address).
+     *
+     * Adding them back is what GPU-direct needs and what host memory must
+     * not have: with the CUDA components live, concurrent host-memory bulk
+     * transfers lose one 4 MiB region per transfer, silently. So this stays
+     * OFF unless NA_UCX_EXTRA_TLS names the components explicitly. Do not
+     * restore a built-in default here; see the header of this patch. */
+    {
+        const char *extra_tls = getenv("NA_UCX_EXTRA_TLS");
+        char        tls_buf[256];
+
+        if (extra_tls != NULL && extra_tls[0] != '\0') {
+            snprintf(tls_buf, sizeof(tls_buf), "%s,%s", tls, extra_tls);
+            status = ucp_config_modify(config, "TLS", tls_buf);
+            /* Fall back to the bare protocol if this UCX build does not know
+             * the requested names, so a typo cannot disable the class. */
+            if (status != UCS_OK) {
+                NA_LOG_SUBSYS_WARNING(cls,
+                    "TLS \"%s\" rejected; falling back to \"%s\"",
+                    tls_buf, tls);
+                status = ucp_config_modify(config, "TLS", tls);
+            }
+        } else {
+            status = ucp_config_modify(config, "TLS", tls);
+        }
+    }
     NA_CHECK_SUBSYS_ERROR(cls, status != UCS_OK, error, ret,
         na_ucs_status_to_na(status), "ucp_config_modify() failed (%s)",
         ucs_status_string(status));
